@startuml
!include ../../../tools/diagrams/kcna-theme.puml
title A kubeconfig is three lists and a pointer

rectangle "clusters — WHERE\n<size:11>server: https://api.prod-east.example.net:6443</size>\n<size:11>certificate-authority-data: base64 of the CA's PUBLIC certificate</size>\n\n<size:11>one entry per API server you talk to</size>" <<accent>> as cl

rectangle "users — WHO (credentials, not identity)\n<size:11>client-certificate-data + client-key-data   ← mTLS, the lab default</size>\n<size:11>OR auth-provider / exec  ← OIDC id-token + refresh-token, enterprise SSO</size>\n<size:11>OR token  ← a ServiceAccount bearer token</size>\n\n<size:11>one entry per credential, NOT per human</size>" <<accent>> as us

rectangle "contexts — WHICH COMBINATION\n<size:11>a context is a POINTER TRIPLE, nothing more:</size>\n<size:11>  cluster: <i>which of the above</i></size>\n<size:11>  user: <i>which of the above</i></size>\n<size:11>  namespace: <i>the default for kubectl when you omit -n</i></size>" <<good>> as ctx

rectangle "current-context — the one in effect\n<size:11>kubectl config use-context prod-east   ← this is all switching clusters means</size>" <<good>> as cur

rectangle "Every name here is an ARBITRARY LABEL you choose\n<size:11>Rename every instance of “default” to “k8s” and the file still works — nothing resolves by name except</size>\n<size:11>the cross-references between these four blocks. That is why a real multi-cluster setup renames them to</size>\n<size:11>something memorable:</size>\n\n<size:11>contexts: prod-east · prod-west · dr-east · dr-west   instead of</size>\n<size:11>          api.mt-d1.na-nw-s01.example.net/user@corp.example.com</size>\n\n<size:11>kubectl config rename-context <ugly> <friendly>     ← one command, and it is permanent</size>" <<plain>> as names

rectangle "One app across four clusters for HA\n<size:11>4 cluster entries · 1 user entry (the SAME SSO credential works everywhere)</size>\n<size:11>· 4 contexts, each pinning its own namespace</size>\n<size:11>kubectl config use-context dr-west, and every later command targets that cluster</size>" <<muted>> as ha

cl -down-> ctx
us -down-> ctx
ctx -down-> cur
cur -down-> names
names -down-> ha

caption <i>kubectl config view redacts the secrets; kubectl config view ~-~-raw shows them, which is how you find the base64 blobs worth decoding. The file merges: set $KUBECONFIG to a colon-separated (semicolon on Windows)</i>\n<i>list and kubectl treats them as one, which is how you keep a work cluster and a lab cluster in separate files without either one clobbering the other.</i>
@enduml
